**Nine sites today with nobody doing anything, against zero.** That is the
argument for building the generated tier before the custom one — and it means
"I need one loaded in there" is already answered nine times over without
ARTIFICIAL having to exist yet. Your own second thought about that is in the
file: *"Maybe we don't need to do this for me."*

## The one structural question, named not answered

A generated site has **no repository and no gitlink**, so it is not a `tenant`
in the sense `sites.yml` means, and not `listed` either because we do build and
publish it. Either the manifest grows a role whose source is a collection entry
rather than a directory, or generation synthesises entries before the manifest
is read.

The hard rule either way: it must never be confusable with `scaffold`. The
template must never reach the public and a generated site must always reach it.

## Also recorded

**Collapsing shows and podcasts.** Your caveat is the real risk — *"I don't know
what that's gonna do to some of our data"* — and I added one you did not
mention: the two collections have different permalinks (`/watch/:name/` and
`/podcasts/:name/`), so a collapse touches live URLs and is a `REDIRECTS.md`
question as much as a data one. It is also **not** a prerequisite for the
generated tier, as long as the generator does not care which collection a show
came from, which it should not.

**`meet` as the listing's first consumer**, as a curated view rather than the
whole list. Nothing to build until the list has entries, which is the generated
tier again.

## Deliberately nothing built

You were winding down and said so. This is one documents-only PR you can merge
without reading closely, or sit on. When you want it, the generated tier is a
well-defined next commit and the count says it is the one worth doing first.

